theory.intro:
A **composite shape** is made by joining or cutting away simple shapes: rectangles, triangles, trapeziums and circle parts. Split it into those simple shapes, find each area, then add or subtract the pieces. Garden beds, floor plans and paths are solved the same way.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(10,0)--(10,3)--(4,3)--(4,6)--(0,6)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,3);
\node at (2,3) {$A_1$};
\node at (7,1.5) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

facts:
A piece **joined on** is added to the total.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(4,8)--(0,5)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,5)--(8,5);
\node at (4,2.5) {$A_1$};
\node at (4,6) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A piece **cut out** — a pool, a doorway, the inside of a border — is subtracted.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (10,6);
\draw[thick] (3,1.5)--(7,1.5)--(5,4.5)--cycle;
\node at (1.5,4.5) {$A_1$};
\node at (5,2.4) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
This block of land is L-shaped.
Calculate its area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(10,0)--(10,3)--(4,3)--(4,6)--(0,6)--cycle;
\node[below] at (5,0) {$10\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (10,1.5) {$3\text{ m}$};
\node[above] at (7,3) {$6\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (4,4.5) {$3\text{ m}$};
\node[above] at (2,6) {$4\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,3) {$6\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(10,0)--(10,3)--(4,3)--(4,6)--(0,6)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,3);
\node at (2,3) {$A_1$};
\node at (7,1.5) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$A_1 = 4\times 6$
$= 24\text{ m}^{2}$
$A_2 = 6\times 3$
$= 18\text{ m}^{2}$
$A = A_1 + A_2$
$= 42\text{ m}^{2}$

foundation.question_text:
The end wall of a shed is a rectangle with a triangle on top.
Calculate its area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(4,8)--(0,5)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,5)--(8,5);
\node[below] at (4,0) {$8\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (8,2.5) {$5\text{ m}$};
\draw[<->] (4.4,5)--(4.4,8) node[midway,right] {$3\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A window is a rectangle with a semicircle joined to the top.
Calculate its area, correct to $2$ decimal places.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.7]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle;
\draw[thick] (6,4) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=3];
\node[below] at (3,0) {$6\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,2) {$4\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A rectangular pool is built into a rectangular yard.
Calculate the area of the yard that is left over (the shaded region), by subtracting the pool.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\fill[blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (10,8);
\fill[white] (2,2) rectangle (8,5);
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (10,8);
\draw[thick] (2,2) rectangle (8,5);
\node[below] at (5,0) {$10\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,4) {$8\text{ m}$};
\node[below] at (5,2) {$6\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (8,3.5) {$3\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

This garden bed is a trapezium.
Calculate its area by splitting it into a rectangle and a triangle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(10,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (6,0)--(6,4);
\node[below] at (5,0) {$10\text{ m}$};
\node[above] at (3,4) {$6\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,2) {$4\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(10,0)--(6,4)--(0,4)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (6,0)--(6,4);
\node at (3,2) {$A_1$};
\node at (7,1.3) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$A_1 = 6\times 4$
$= 24\text{ m}^{2}$
$A_2 = \frac{1}{2}\times 4\times 4$
$= 8\text{ m}^{2}$
$A = A_1 + A_2$
$= 32\text{ m}^{2}$

foundation.question_text:
A triangular flower bed is dug into a rectangular lawn.
Calculate the area of grass that is left (the shaded region), by subtracting the flower bed.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.5]
\fill[blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (12,8);
\fill[white] (3,1)--(9,1)--(6,5)--cycle;
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(12,0) node[midway,below] {$12\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] (12,0)--(12,8)--(0,8);
\draw[thick] (0,8)--(0,0) node[midway,left] {$8\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] (3,1)--(9,1) node[midway,below] {$6\text{ m}$};
\draw[thick] (9,1)--(6,5)--(3,1);
\draw[dashed] (6,1)--(6,5) node[midway,right] {$4\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

This concrete slab is T-shaped.
Calculate its area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw[thick] (3,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(9,4)--(9,7)--(0,7)--(0,4)--(3,4)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,4)--(9,4);
\node[above] at (4.5,7) {$9\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,5.5) {$3\text{ m}$};
\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$3\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (6,2) {$4\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.55]
\draw[thick] (3,0)--(6,0)--(6,4)--(9,4)--(9,7)--(0,7)--(0,4)--(3,4)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (0,4)--(9,4);
\node at (4.5,5.5) {$A_1$};
\node at (4.5,2) {$A_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$A_1 = 9\times 3$
$= 27\text{ m}^{2}$
$A_2 = 3\times 4$
$= 12\text{ m}^{2}$
$A = A_1 + A_2$
$= 39\text{ m}^{2}$

foundation.question_text:
This timber decking is L-shaped.
Calculate its area.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(9,0)--(9,3)--(4,3)--(4,5)--(0,5)--cycle;
\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$9\text{ m}$};
\node[right] at (9,1.5) {$3\text{ m}$};
\node[above] at (2,5) {$4\text{ m}$};
\node[left] at (0,2.5) {$5\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.6]
\draw[thick] (0,0)--(9,0)--(9,3)--(4,3)--(4,5)--(0,5)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,3);
\node at (2,2.5) {$A_1$};
